---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Friends' sites, borrowed from Ben Katz's list at robot-daycare.com/about
const friends = [
  { name: 'Aaron Sliski', url: 'https://www.aaronsliski.com/index.html' },
  { name: 'Alex Hattori', url: 'https://www.alex-hattori.com/blog' },
  { name: 'Amy Qian', url: 'https://amymakesstuff.com/' },
  { name: 'Andrew Birkel', url: 'https://andrewbirkel.com/project_list.html' },
  { name: 'Austin Brown', url: 'https://brushless.zone/' },
  { name: 'Ava Chen', url: 'https://www.avamakesthings.com/' },
  { name: 'Bayley Wang', url: 'https://isopack.blogspot.com/' },
  { name: 'Charles Guan', url: 'https://www.etotheipiplusone.net' },
  { name: 'Dane Kouttron', url: 'https://transistor-man.com/' },
  { name: 'Jared Di Carlo', url: 'https://jareddicarlo.com/' },
  { name: 'Nancy Ouyang', url: 'https://orangenarwhals.com/' },
  { name: 'Shane Colton', url: 'https://scolton.blogspot.com/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Background, what I'm focused on now, and the longer-term thing I'm building toward."
  wide={false}
>
  <nav class="subnav mono" aria-label="On this page">
    <a class="plain" href="#about">About</a>
    <a class="plain" href="#now">Now</a>
    <a class="plain" href="#vision">Vision</a>
    <a class="plain" href="#friends">Friends</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="about">
    <p class="pill">About</p>
    <h1>Background</h1>
    <!-- TODO: replace with your real story: undergrad at VIT Vellore, how you found
         legged robotics, the path to Stochastic Robotics Lab, what pulled you in. -->
    <p>
      I did my undergrad at VIT Vellore, where a mix of robotics coursework and a stubborn fascination with highly agile
      and dynamic systems sparked my interest in legged robotics. That turned into work on quadrupeds and bipeds: actuators,
      model-based control, trajectory optimization, and co-design frameworks. I now do research at the <strong>Stochastic Robotics Lab</strong> at IISc, led by Prof. Shishir Kolathaya.
    </p>
    <p>
      What keeps me here: legs are a hard control problem wearing a simple costume.
      Everything (contact, uncertainty, real hardware that doesn't care about your
      assumptions) shows up the moment a robot tries to take a step.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="now">
    <p class="pill">Now</p>
    <h2>What I'm focused on this month</h2>
    <p class="muted mono small">Last updated: <!-- TODO: keep this current --> August 2026</p>
    <!-- TODO: replace with your actual current focus: e.g. the intern → RA
         transition, a specific research thread (contact-implicit MPC, whole-body
         control), a paper you're finishing. Update this every few weeks; it's the
         thing that signals the site is alive. -->
    <p>
      Right now I am spending most of my time setting up the xml generator for parametric biped design.
      The goal is to deploy various design iterations on the same MPC control stack. My other project is to design a scout rover 
      for ISRO working under Dr.Shishir N.Y and Dr.Jishnu Keshavan 
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      This section is meant to go stale on purpose and then get rewritten;
      that's the point. Check the date above.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="vision">
    <p class="pill">Vision</p>
    <h2>What I'm building toward</h2>
    <!-- TODO: this is the section almost nobody has: make it real. Replace with
         your actual long-horizon thing, e.g. eventually founding an independent
         research institute focused on legged robotics / embodied intelligence. -->
    <p>
      The longer arc: I'd like to eventually start an independent research lab,
      small, opinionated, not beholden to a single funding cycle or publication
      treadmill, built around the belief that embodied intelligence (robots that
      actually have to deal with gravity, contact, and friction) is understudied
      relative to how important it is. Everything I'm doing now, from the actuator
      testbenches to the control theory and AI, is scaffolding toward being able to
      run something like that credibly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="friends">
    <p class="pill">Friends</p>
    <h2>Other people's sites worth reading</h2>
    <p>
      Sites by makers and roboticists, taken from the list on{' '}
      <a href="https://robot-daycare.com/about/">Robot Daycare</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="friends">
      {friends.map((f) => (
        <li><a href={f.url}>{f.name}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <blockquote class="quote">
    <p>
      "A human being should be able to change a diaper, plan an invasion, butcher a hog,
      conn a ship, design a building, write a sonnet, balance accounts, build a wall, set a
      bone, comfort the dying, take orders, give orders, cooperate, act alone, solve
      equations, analyze a new problem, pitch manure, program a computer, cook a tasty
      meal, fight efficiently, die gallantly. Specialization is for insects."
    </p>
    <cite>Robert A. Heinlein</cite>
  </blockquote>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .subnav {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .subnav a {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
  }
  .subnav a:hover {
    color: var(--accent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .small {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
  }
  .friends {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    columns: 2;
    column-gap: 2rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .friends {
      columns: 1;
    }
  }
  .quote {
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .quote p {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
    margin: 0 auto 0.6rem;
  }
  .quote cite {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
